      "writtenOn": "2025-10-13T15:09:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm... we free the memcache used for all vCPUs, not just this one? This assumes, at least, that kvm_x86_call(vcpu_free)() will never be called for multiple vCPUs in parallel?\n\nIt seems better to avoid such tricky assumptions.\n\n...It feels we should eventually (or maybe now?) get rid of using memcache for this stuff (i.e. for freeing pages that were allocated _not_ via memcache). As we discussed earlier, using it for pages allocated via alloc_pages_exact() is potentially incorrect, or at least is unclean and hacky.\n\nAnd, in case of pkvm_vcpu pages, the host knows exactly how many pages to free anyway (pkvm_vcpu_sz \u003d PAGE_ALIGN(PKVM_SHADOW_VCPU_STATE_SIZE)), so using memcache seems like a complete overkill? we can easily just use free_pages_exact()?\n\nAnd in case of cpuid_entries pages it also seems easy: the host knows the current value of vcpu-\u003earch.cpuid_nent, right?",
      "revId": "e21a4a1ba760ff1bd5956185b9532b87e6b0f1ef",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "46951c62_5bcaff17",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm_high.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 922,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-14T01:51:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Hmm... we free the memcache used for all vCPUs, not just this one? This assumes, at least, that kvm_x86_call(vcpu_free)() will never be called for multiple vCPUs in parallel?\n\nThe current vcpu_free ops usage in the KVM is not in parallel. The KVM code calls the vcpu_free ops one by one in function `kvm_destroy_vcpus`. So it frees the memcache for vcpus one by one.\n\n\u003e And, in case of pkvm_vcpu pages, the host knows exactly how many pages to free anyway (pkvm_vcpu_sz \u003d PAGE_ALIGN(PKVM_SHADOW_VCPU_STATE_SIZE)), so using memcache seems like a complete overkill? we can easily just use free_pages_exact()?\nAnd in case of cpuid_entries pages it also seems easy: the host knows the current value of vcpu-\u003earch.cpuid_nent, right?\n\nThen we need a mechanism to return two physical addresses with the current kvm_call_pkvm implementation, one is for pkvm_vcpu and the other is for cpuid_entries. Not sure if you have any easy idea for this?\n\nIn v6.15, I have extended the memcache with a memory size element. For the physical address with 4K size, it is freed via free_page(). Otherwise, it is freed via free_pages_exact(). And the kvm_call_pkvm is also extended to return multiple outputs, so the vcpu_free PV interface can return a structure pkvm_memcache and doesn\u0027t need to have a pkvm_memcache in kvm structure, which can also support vcpu_free PV interface being use in parallel.\nBut for v6.12, I am not sure if it is really necessary to do these refactors as seems without these refactors, v6.12 can also work without any issue (vcpu_free PV interface is used in serial, and free_pages_exact() is implemented via calling free_page() one by one).",
      "parentUuid": "8caadb46_c58bb1eb",
      "revId": "e21a4a1ba760ff1bd5956185b9532b87e6b0f1ef",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1469dbf6_7aae037f",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm_high.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 922,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-14T10:03:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e The current vcpu_free ops usage in the KVM is not in parallel. The KVM code calls the vcpu_free ops one by one in function kvm_destroy_vcpus. So it frees the memcache for vcpus one by one.\n\nYes, but why deliberately make ourselves dependent on the assumption that it will always be the case? That might be fine as a temporary solution for 6.12 only, but for 6.15+ we probably want a cleaner and more robust solution.\n\n\u003e Then we need a mechanism to return two physical addresses with the current kvm_call_pkvm implementation, one is for pkvm_vcpu and the other is for cpuid_entries.\n\nOk, I forgot that KVM doesn\u0027t remember the addresses of pkvm_vcpu and cpuid_entries that it allocates and passes to pKVM.\n\nBut it could remember them, right? So why not just do that?\n\n\u003e In v6.15, I have extended the memcache with a memory size element. [...]\n\nAgain, do we really _need_ to use memcache for freeing pages that were not allocated via memcache? Does that really have any advantages?",
      "parentUuid": "46951c62_5bcaff17",
      "revId": "e21a4a1ba760ff1bd5956185b9532b87e6b0f1ef",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d61a0253_4f477ea5",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm_high.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 922,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-14T10:28:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Yes, but why deliberately make ourselves dependent on the assumption that it will always be the case? That might be fine as a temporary solution for 6.12 only, but for 6.15+ we probably want a cleaner and more robust solution.\n\nYes, agree.\n\n\u003e But it could remember them, right? So why not just do that?\n\nYeah, it would be remembered if we extend the kvm_vcpu with two more elements to remember these two address. That will be independent with the return value. And when adds fpu, we probably need one more to record the fpu memory address passed to the pkvm. These seems makes things a bit complex than using `free_pkvm_memcache` as we don\u0027t need to change anything when adds fpu.\n\n\u003eAgain, do we really need to use memcache for freeing pages that were not allocated via memcache? Does that really have any advantages?\n\nSo you are expecting only the memory pages passed to the pkvm via memcache can be returned via memcache. If some memory pages are passed via the hypercall, it should not be returned via the memcache?\n\nThe benifit of memcache is to return multiple physical addresses. For example, for vcpu, we will have 3 independent physical addresses, pkvm_vcpu, cpuid_entries and fpu. Of cause we can return 3 of them by the kvm_call_pkvm() in v6.15 as kvm_call_pkvm() can support 4 outputs. But if there will be more, for example, a vcpu is not freed by vcpu_free interface, and the vm_destroy PV interfaces will free all the un-freed vcpu, then there will be more than 3 physical addressed to be returned, then extending kvm_call_pkvm() to support more output is not working. The way you suggested, to let the host to remember these addresses still works (but need to introduce additional elements for this purpose). With using memcache, the kvm_call_pkvm() just needs to return fixed number of outputs, which is the memcache structure, and the memcache can store as many physical addresses as we want. We can put the pkvm_vcpu\u0027s PA + size in memcache, then put cpuid_entries\u0027s PA + size inside pkvm_vcpu\u0027s memory, and put fpu\u0027s PA + size in cpuid_entries\u0027s memory, and so on to extend if there will be more.",
      "parentUuid": "1469dbf6_7aae037f",
      "revId": "e21a4a1ba760ff1bd5956185b9532b87e6b0f1ef",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b2c0abb7_d0d9c641",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm_high.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 922,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-14T11:54:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Yeah, it would be remembered if we extend the kvm_vcpu with two more elements to remember these two address. That will be independent with the return value. And when adds fpu, we probably need one more to record the fpu memory address passed to the pkvm. These seems makes things a bit complex than using free_pkvm_memcache as we don\u0027t need to change anything when adds fpu.\n\nIt doesn\u0027t seem very complex to me. Currently `struct kvm_vcpu_arch` has `pkvm_vcpu_handle` as the only pkvm-specific field, we could instead define a `struct kvm_protected_vcpu pkvm` field (similarly to `struct kvm_protected_vm pkvm` in `struct kvm_arch`), which would contain the pkvm_vcpu_handle as well as those remembered addresses of pkvm_vcpu, cpuid_entries and fpstate. When we add something other feature that requires allocating and donating memory to hypervisor, we just add another field to that structure. That\u0027s the most straightforward and obvious approach I can think of.\n\n(BTW actually `struct kvm_protected_vm` is a misnomer, since it is not just for protected VMs. So we\u0027d better name it e.g. `struct kvm_pkvm_vm`. And then, correspondingly, `struct kvm_pkvm_vcpu`.)\n\nAlthough a potential problem with this approach is Android\u0027s \"KMI freeze\" policy. Extending `struct kvm_vcpu_arch` probably changes the ABI for vendor modules, so we may not always be able to do that in the given Android kernel version.\n\n\u003e The benifit of memcache is to return multiple physical addresses. [...]\n\nIf we remember the allocated addresses in struct kvm_vcpu_arch, we don\u0027t need to return them at all.\n\nAnyway, I agree that using memcache is more flexible, so we might prefer that.\n\nIf so, I imagine we\u0027d want to have `teardown_mc` per vCPU, not just per VM. So that we can freely use it, in particular, for multiple vCPUs in parallel independently of each other, not just in pkvm_vcpu_free() but also in pkvm_vcpu_after_set_cpuid() and pkvm_vcpu_realloc_fpstate() (why not? it would make things easier for KVM-high, which would need to do literally just `free_pkvm_memcache(\u0026vcpu-\u003earch.pkvm.teardown_mc)` in all those cases).",
      "parentUuid": "d61a0253_4f477ea5",
      "revId": "e21a4a1ba760ff1bd5956185b9532b87e6b0f1ef",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b3db7314_e9823a98",
